## 4. Current results (BioMistral-7B, test split, seed 0)

| Variant | Accuracy (strict) | Sel. Acc. | Causal Consistency | Violation Rate | Coverage |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| (1) Base LLM | 0.594 | 0.594 | 0.234 | 0.109 | 1.000 |
| (2) + RAG | 0.633 | 0.633 | 0.281 | 0.109 | 1.000 |
| (3) + Symbolic Gate | 0.820 | 0.820 | 0.641 | 0.094 | 1.000 |
| (4) + UQ Engine | 0.562 | 1.000 | 0.562 | 0.000 | 0.562 |

Paired McNemar (item-level correctness, Holm-corrected):

| Comparison | B01 | B10 | p (Holm) |
|---|---|---|---|
| base→rag | 12 | 7 | 0.359 — **not significant** |
| rag→nsai | 24 | 0 | 2.4e-07 — **strongly significant** |
| nsai→nsai_uq | 0 | 33 | 7.0e-10 — significant but **in the wrong direction** |

Held-out split (16 pairs only, wide CIs) reproduces the same pattern:
`rag→nsai` p=0.008 with 8 improvements and 0 regressions.

### Other measured facts

- Base model arm-wise accuracy: **UNSAFE arm 0.891, SAFE arm 0.297**. The model
  calls almost everything unsafe. This is why pair-level Causal Consistency is
  the right metric and plain accuracy is misleading.
- Symbolic gate fires on 72/128 (56%) of test cases, declines on the rest,
  and makes **0 incorrect** decisions when it fires.
- Entropy AUROC as a wrongness detector on gate-declined cases: **0.374**.
  Below 0.5 — the signal is *inverted*.

---

## 5. Bugs already found and fixed — DO NOT REINTRODUCE

1. **`endswith("safe")` matches `"unsafe"` too.** Used twice in throwaway
   analysis snippets and produced n=128 where n=64 was correct, hiding the true
   SAFE-arm accuracy. Always split arms on the `arm` field, not the id suffix.

2. **Age contradiction in implicit vignettes.** For the `aspirin_reye` family
   the causal factor *is* age, but the implicit template wrote a generic patient
   age (71) in the opening sentence while the true causal value was 14. Four
   records had ground truth contradicting their own text. Fixed in
   `hardening.py::render` via `stated_age`. Any new family whose causal variable
   also appears in the vignette boilerplate needs the same treatment.

3. **UQ threshold calibrated on the wrong population.** `tau` was fitted over
   all calibration items including those the symbolic gate decides
   deterministically. Their entropies carry no information about neural
   uncertainty, which corrupted `tau` and collapsed the abstention set onto
   exactly the non-gated cases (abstained=56, of which gate_fired=0;
   answered=72, of which gate_fired=72 — perfect degeneracy). Fixed: `tau` is
   now calibrated only on gate-declined calibration items.

4. **Selective vs. strict accuracy conflated.** Row 4 originally showed
   accuracy 1.000 at coverage 0.562 because accuracy was computed over answered
   items only. Both are now reported; `accuracy` (strict, abstention counts as
   wrong) is the row-comparable one.

Also note: the benchmark was **hardened after seeing the first result** because
the gate saturated at 1.000. This is legitimate (it made the task harder, not
easier) but must be disclosed in any write-up. `--no_hardening` reproduces the
original.

---

## 6. Open problems, in priority order

### P1 — UQ signal is inverted — **INVESTIGATED, see `results/p1_uq_signals.md`**

Status: acceptance criterion met, negative result confirmed for the `nsai_uq`
row, plus one positive finding. Remaining: self-consistency (k=5) not attempted.

- Entropy has no signal on any population (AUROC 0.374–0.469). Replicated.
- `logit_margin` (logit(SAFE) − logit(UNSAFE) at the decision token) is
  **reliably better than entropy** — paired bootstrap on identical items gives
  +0.199 (p=0.004) on base, +0.119 (p=0.004) on rag, +0.170 (p=0.016) on the
  gate-declined pool. Same sign on held-out but underpowered.
- But on the population the UQ engine actually governs (gate-declined, n=56) the
  best signal is `answer_logprob` at 0.560 with 95% CI [0.406, 0.714]. The CI
  covers chance, so it does **not** clear the bar. No thresholds were tuned.
- Structural point worth writing up: the gate decides 72/128 with 0 errors,
  removing exactly the determinable cases. A signal at AUROC 0.668 on the full
  population falls to 0.544 on the residual. The gate and the UQ engine compete
  for the same easy cases.

Tooling added: `uq_diagnostic.py --signal {entropy,max_entropy,logit_margin,
answer_logprob}`, `--all` for a comparison table with bootstrap CIs, and
`--paired A B` for a paired bootstrap of the AUROC difference.

**Tokenizer trap — do not reintroduce.** On this SPM tokenizer `" SAFE"` and
`" UNSAFE"` both start with the bare-space token 28705, so `encode(x)[0]` puts
one id in both answer classes and makes the margin meaningless. `model.py::
_answer_token_ids` skips leading whitespace-only pieces and drops any still
ambiguous id. One eval run was discarded to this bug.

### P1 (original description)

Token-level predictive entropy does not detect wrong answers on this task.
AUROC 0.374; mean entropy is *higher* on correct answers (1.07) than wrong ones
(0.91); the risk-coverage curve rises rather than falls as coverage drops
(error 0.411 at coverage 1.00 → 0.545 at coverage 0.20).

This falsifies the assumption in §4.7 of the proposal. That is a legitimate
finding and should be reported as such. But before settling for it, try
alternative uncertainty signals that are tied to the *decision* rather than the
whole vocabulary distribution:

- **Logit margin** between the `SAFE` and `UNSAFE` answer tokens at the first
  generated position. Most promising — it measures uncertainty about the actual
  binary decision.
- Sequence log-probability of the answer token only, not averaged over the
  justification.
- Self-consistency: sample k=5 completions at temperature>0 and use answer
  disagreement rate. Note this breaks the deterministic-decoding guarantee, so
  it needs its own variant row rather than replacing the existing one.

**Acceptance criterion:** report AUROC of the new signal via
`src/uq_diagnostic.py` (extend it to accept a `--signal` argument). If AUROC
stays below 0.55, stop and report the negative result — do not tune thresholds
until a number looks good.

### P2 — Seeds do not produce any variance

`do_sample=False` makes decoding deterministic, so all 5 seeds return identical
numbers and the table shows `± 0.000`. Reporting "5 seeds" with zero std implies
variability was measured when it was not.

Fix: make the seed affect **dataset construction** (patient ages, which value
from the near-threshold list is chosen, which distractor labs are injected)
rather than decoding. Then std means "sensitivity to benchmark instantiation",
which is a real and defensible quantity.

Implementation sketch: `build_dataset.py --seed N` already exists but the seed
is not actually used in value selection (`idx % len(values)` is deterministic).
Route the `rng` through value selection and age/sex assignment, emit
`data/seed{N}/`, and let `run_eval.py` take `--data data/seed{N}`.

**Acceptance criterion:** non-zero std in the table; the qualitative ordering of
rows 1–3 must survive.

### P3 — RAG does not work — **RESOLVED, see `results/p3_retrieval.md`**

Acceptance criterion met. Stated hit rate: **hit@3 = 0.648 on test, 1.000 on
held-out** (`src/retrieval_diagnostic.py`, new).

Retrieval is not the bottleneck. On held-out the correct guideline was retrieved
for every case at ceiling precision, and Causal Consistency was still 0.000. On
test, accuracy is 0.614 when the gold doc is retrieved vs 0.667 when it is not —
no relationship in either direction. The model ignores retrieved context.
**"RAG is insufficient on this benchmark" is now defensible.** A dense retriever
would raise the test hit rate but held-out shows it would not change the
conclusion.

### P3 (original description)

`base→rag` is not significant on the test split (p=0.36) and Causal Consistency
*drops* to 0.000 on held-out. Two independent observations of the same pattern.

Before reporting "RAG does not help", verify it is not a plumbing failure:

- What is the retrieval hit rate? For each case, does the correct guideline for
  that rule family appear in the top-k? `results/preds_*_rag_*.jsonl` stores the
  retrieved doc ids in the `retrieved` field — measure precision@3 against
  `guideline::{family}`.
- If retrieval hit rate is high but accuracy does not move, the model is
  ignoring the context, which is a genuine and reportable finding.
- If retrieval hit rate is low, TF-IDF is the problem — try a dense retriever
  before drawing conclusions.

**Acceptance criterion:** a stated retrieval hit rate. The claim "RAG is
insufficient" is only defensible once retrieval is shown to have worked.

### P4 — Clinical rules are hand-written

`src/rules.py` thresholds were written from memory, not sourced. The proposal
requires the SCM to be "initialized from biomedical ontologies (e.g., UMLS) and
refined using expert-curated causal relations" (§4.2).

`src/fetch_openfda.py` exists and is designed to pull real FDA label text
(`https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json`, fields `contraindications`,
`boxed_warning`, `warnings_and_precautions`, `drug_interactions`, plus
`openfda.rxcui` / `openfda.spl_set_id`) and emit a curation worksheet where a
human transcribes the numeric threshold from the quoted source sentence.

**Status update — it HAS now been run against the live API.** Outputs:
`data/openfda_raw.jsonl` (10 labels) and `data/curation_worksheet.csv`
(145 rows, 8 drugs: lisinopril, metformin, nitrofurantoin, ondansetron,
propranolol, simvastatin, spironolactone, warfarin). 82 of the 145 rows contain
a number. Two of the ten drugs in `drugs.txt` returned nothing and are missing
from the worksheet.

The open question is now answered in part: FDA labels **do** sometimes state
explicit numeric thresholds — e.g. metformin, "Severe renal impairment: (eGFR
below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2)". Whether that holds for all ten families is unknown
until a human reads the 82 numeric rows.

**Blocking gap: `constraint_value` is empty on all 145 rows.** Nobody has
curated the worksheet yet, so `src/rules.py` is still running on the
hand-written thresholds. Until a human transcribes the numbers, P4 is not
closed. Do not invent numbers to fill the gap, and do not regex numbers out of
prose that may refer to something else.

Deeper sources also pending: UMLS/UTS account (free, registration takes days),
MIMIC-IV via PhysioNet (requires CITI training, takes weeks — start the
application now if it has not been started). Note the **MIMIC-IV Clinical
Database Demo v2.2 is open access with no credentialing** (ODbL, 100 patients,
all 26 tables including `labevents` and `prescriptions`, but no free-text
notes) — usable immediately while the full application is pending.

### P5 — The symbolic gate is not Aim 3

The current gate is a **post-hoc** check on the model's output. Aim 3 specifies
a Constraint-Aware Layer that modifies intermediate hidden states before
decoding: `h'_l = h_l + α·P_causal(h_l)`, with joint objective
`L = L_LM + λ1·L_ontology + λ2·L_causal + λ3·L_uncertainty`.

The current implementation is a reasonable stand-in for a week-one ablation but
must not be presented as the proposal's intervention layer. When the real layer
is built, `src/perplexity.py` gives the pre-intervention baseline needed to show
general language capability is not degraded (RQ3).

### P6 — Aims 1 and 2 not started

SAE training on middle-layer activations, Feature Interpretability Score
(`FIS = α·S_semantic + β·S_causal + γ·S_human`), sparse concept probing
classifiers, then knock-out/injection experiments with Average Causal Effect and
negative controls. Fallback to JumpReLU or TopK SAE if standard SAEs yield <30%
expert-validated features. This is the bulk of the 36-month project and needs
more GPU than a single 4090 for the SAE stage.

---

## 7. Non-negotiables

These were deliberate decisions. Do not "optimise" them away.

- **No quantisation.** bf16 only. 4-bit perturbs the logit distribution and
  invalidates any entropy- or logit-based uncertainty measure.
- **No LLM-as-judge and no human annotation** for ground truth. Labels are
  generated programmatically from the rule definitions.
- **Causal Consistency is pair-level** — both arms of a counterfactual pair must
  be correct. A model that always answers UNSAFE would score 50% per-arm but 0%
  at pair level, which is the entire point.
- **Paired statistics.** Identical prompts, decoding, item order and seeds
  across variants so McNemar is valid. Holm correction across the three
  consecutive comparisons.
- **The gate never guesses.** If it cannot extract the relevant fact it defers
  to the neural answer. Do not add fallback heuristics that make it fire more
  often — its 0-error record depends on this.
- **Never report MockLM numbers.** `MockLM` exists only to test plumbing without
  a GPU. `make_table.py` prints a warning banner when the backend is mock.
- **Do not tune thresholds until a number looks good.** If a signal has no
  discriminative power, report that.

